\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.3}{\ignorespaces How many years of return data are needed before a strategy's track record becomes statistically significant. The years count the length of the history being analyzed, not how long the strategy must trade or hold its positions. With a true Sharpe ratio of 0.5, about 15 years of data are needed to reach $t = 1.96$. With a Sharpe ratio of 0.25, about 61 years are needed.}}{16}{figure.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:01-years}{{1.3}{16}{How many years of return data are needed before a strategy's track record becomes statistically significant. The years count the length of the history being analyzed, not how long the strategy must trade or hold its positions. With a true Sharpe ratio of 0.5, about 15 years of data are needed to reach $t = 1.96$. With a Sharpe ratio of 0.25, about 61 years are needed}{figure.caption.8}{}}
